The '''Eurovision Song Contest 2005''' was the 50th edition of the [[Eurovision Song Contest]]. It consisted of a semi-final on 19 May and a final on 21 May 2005, held at the [[Palace of Sports (Kyiv)|Palace of Sports]] in [[Kyiv]], Ukraine, and presented by [[Maria Efrosinina]] and [[Pavlo Shylko]]. It was organised by the [[European Broadcasting Union]] (EBU) and host broadcaster [[National Television Company of Ukraine]] (NTU), who staged the event after winning the {{Escyr|2004||2004 contest}} for {{esccnty|Ukraine|y=2004}} with the song "[[Wild Dances]]" by [[Ruslana]].

Broadcasters from thirty-nine countries participated in the contest, three more than the previous record of thirty-six, that took part the year before. {{esccnty|Bulgaria}} and {{esccnty|Moldova}} made their first participation this year, while {{esccnty|Hungary}} returned to the contest after a six-year absence, having last taken part {{esccnty|Hungary|y=1998|t=in 1998}}.

The winner was {{Esccnty|Greece|y=2005}} with the song "[[My Number One]]", performed by [[Helena Paparizou]] and written by Manos Psaltakis, [[Christos Dantis]] and Natalia Germanou. This was Greece's first victory in the contest after 31 years of participation. {{esccnty|Malta|y=2005}}, {{esccnty|Romania|y=2005}}, {{esccnty|Israel|y=2005}}, and {{esccnty|Latvia|y=2005}} rounded out the top five. Malta equalled their best result from {{Escyr|2002}}, while Romania achieved their best result in their Eurovision history. Unusually, all "[[Big Four (Eurovision)|Big Four]]" countries (France, Germany, Spain and United Kingdom) ended up as the "Last Four", all placing in the bottom four positions in the final.

==Location==
[[File:Kyiv Palace of Sports.jpg|thumb|left|Palace of Sports, Kyiv – host venue pictured during the 2005 contest.]]
[[Kyiv]] is the capital and largest city of Ukraine, located in the north central part of the country on the [[Dnieper]]. The Palace of Sports, a multi-purpose indoor arena, was confirmed by officials as the host venue on 6 September 2004.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://esctoday.com/3102/ntu_and_ebu_confirm_palats_sportu/|title=Eurovision NTU and EBU confirm: Palats Sportu - ESCToday.com|date=6 September 2004|access-date=8 April 2018|archive-date=19 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201019181823/http://esctoday.com/3102/ntu_and_ebu_confirm_palats_sportu/|url-status=live}}</ref> However, in order to host the contest, the facilities had been brought up to the standard required by the [[European Broadcasting Union]] (EBU).

At the end of December 2004, work began on the renovation of the hall,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://esctoday.com/3533/renovation_of_palats_sportu_has_begun/|title=Eurovision Renovation of Palats Sportu has begun|date=26 December 2004|publisher=ESCToday.com|access-date=8 April 2018|archive-date=6 November 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201106034551/http://esctoday.com/3533/renovation_of_palats_sportu_has_begun/|url-status=live}}</ref> for which approximately 4 million francs were allocated.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://esctoday.com/4311/ntu_president_doubts_about_palats_sportu_expenses/|title=Eurovision NTU President doubts about Palats Sportu expenses|date=27 April 2005|publisher=ESCToday.com|access-date=8 April 2018|archive-date=22 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201022213549/http://esctoday.com/4311/ntu_president_doubts_about_palats_sportu_expenses/|url-status=live}}</ref> Renovation works were to be finished by 20 April,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://esctoday.com/3910/palats_sportu_must_be_finished_by_20_april/|title=Eurovision 'Palats Sportu must be finished by 20 April'|date=27 February 2005|publisher=ESCToday.com|access-date=8 April 2018|archive-date=23 January 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210123192342/http://esctoday.com/3910/palats_sportu_must_be_finished_by_20_april/|url-status=live}}</ref> however, they were completed at the beginning of May.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://esctoday.com/4362/kyiv_the_stage_is_taking_shape/|title=Eurovision Kyiv: The stage is taking shape|date=4 May 2005|publisher=ESCToday.com|access-date=8 April 2018|archive-date=23 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201023104558/http://esctoday.com/4362/kyiv_the_stage_is_taking_shape/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://esctoday.com/4444/palats_sportu_scene_is_getting_ready/|title=Eurovision Palats Sportu scene is getting ready|date=11 May 2005|publisher=ESCToday.com|access-date=8 April 2018|archive-date=23 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201023135806/http://esctoday.com/4444/palats_sportu_scene_is_getting_ready/|url-status=live}}</ref> The arena could accommodate over 5,000 seated spectators. Additionally 2,000 press delegates were catered for.

Hotel rooms were scarce as the contest organisers asked the [[Government of Ukraine|Ukrainian government]] to put a block on bookings they did not control themselves through official delegation allocations or tour packages: this led to many people's hotel bookings being cancelled.<ref>{{cite web |first=John |last=Marone |publisher=The Ukrainian Observer |url=http://www.ukraine-observer.com/articles/208/655 |title=Where Do We Put The Foreign Tourists? |access-date=18 July 2006 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060204201731/http://www.ukraine-observer.com/articles/208/655 |archive-date=4 February 2006 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

Organizers hoped that by hosting Eurovision, it would boost Ukraine's image abroad and increase [[tourism]], while the country's new [[Government of Ukraine|government]] hoped that it would also give a modest boost to the long-term goal of acquiring [[European Union]] membership.

==Participants==
{{further|List of countries in the Eurovision Song Contest}}
{{ESC 2005 participants}}
Thirty-nine countries participated in the 2005 contest. {{esccnty|Hungary}} returned to the contest after a six-year absence, last competing {{esccnty|Hungary|y=1998|t=in 1998}}. {{esccnty|Bulgaria}} and {{esccnty|Moldova}} competed in the contest for the first time. {{esccnty|Czech Republic}} and {{esccnty|Lebanon}} were originally announced as participants, but the former withdrew before the deadline, and the latter withdrew in March 2005, two months before the contest.

Several of the performing artists had previously represented the same country in past editions: [[Constantinos Christoforou]] had represented {{esccnty|Cyprus|y=1996|t=Cyprus in 1996}} as a solo artist and {{esccnty|Cyprus|y=2002|t=in 2002}} as part of [[One (band)|One]]; [[Helena Paparizou]] had represented {{esccnty|Greece|y=2001|t=Greece in 2001}} as part of [[Antique (duo)|Antique]]; [[Selma Björnsdóttir|Selma]] had represented {{esccnty|Iceland|y=1999|t=Iceland in 1999}}; and [[Chiara Siracusa|Chiara]] had represented {{esccnty|Malta|y=1998|t=Malta in 1998}}. In addition, [[Alexandros Panayi]] providing backing vocals for Greece, had represented {{esccnty|Cyprus|y=1995|t=Cyprus in 1995}} as a solo artist and {{esccnty|Cyprus|y=2000|t=in 2000}} as part of [[Voice (duo)|Voice]]; [[Anabel Conde]] providing backing vocals for Andorra, had represented {{esccnty|Spain|y=1995|t=Spain in 1995}}; and [[Elina Konstantopoulou]] providing backing vocals for Cyprus, had represented {{esccnty|Greece|y=1995|t=Greece in 1995}}.

